## The Ultimate Safe Function

Combine `requires`, `ensures`, `&`, and `&mut` for maximum safety:

```xiom
type Account = {
  owner: Str;
  balance: Float64;
}

fn transfer(from: &mut Account, to: &mut Account, amount: Float64)
  requires: amount > 0.0;
  requires: amount <= from.balance;
  ensures: from.balance == from@pre.balance - amount;
  ensures: to.balance == to@pre.balance + amount;
{
  from.balance = from.balance - amount;
  to.balance = to.balance + amount;
}
```

### What This Guarantees

- **Contracts**: Can't transfer negative amounts, can't overdraft, balances balance
- **Ownership**: `&mut` ensures nobody else can read or write the accounts during transfer

### Layered Safety

```xiom
fn withdraw(account: &mut Account, amount: Float64)
  requires: amount > 0.0;
  requires: amount <= account.balance;
{
  account.balance = account.balance - amount;
}

fn deposit(account: &mut Account, amount: Float64)
  requires: amount > 0.0;
{
  account.balance = account.balance + amount;
}
```

### Contracts + Ownership in Practice

Contracts validate LOGIC (is this operation valid?).
Ownership validates MEMORY (can this value be accessed?).
Together: no logic bugs AND no memory bugs.

